\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The Sages left the stage the way they had come to it, which is to say the stage was one man again and nobody on the lawn could have named the moment the ukuleles stopped being there. The High Elvis crossed to the single microphone at the centre mark, and this time he lifted it. He held it a while in his folded hands, the way he had held the silence at the start, and let half a million people climb up out of the great emptied quiet the solo had abandoned them in and settle into the one he was keeping instead. From the platform Sarah watched him do it. She had watched him do it all afternoon to smaller rooms \'97 a punch-up here, a stage invasion there, each talked gently down off its ledge with the patience of a man reading a gas meter \'97 and she recognised the manoeuvre even as it closed over her, which is the only honest thing you can say about a good one.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 When he spoke it was not the voice from the Inauguration. A part of the crowd waited for that one \'97 the shaken-up, weak-kneed, burning-love ceremonial roll, the line thrown out to be caught, Praise the Dawkins flung at a screen and flung back \'97 and it did not come. What came was smaller and closer, a man talking over a fence at dusk to one neighbour he happened to like. He told them they were the lucky ones. He told them the whole of them, every cape and sideburn and structurally-cream cocktail out there in the dark, had come up out of nothing at all by the one long blind accident that had been running since before there was a Ghetto to run it in, and that the name they had given the accident, when they finally got round to loving it, was the One. He said the One had a way of not stopping. He said there was another coming after \'97 the One After the One \'97 and that the Holy Momma was carrying Him out in front of her this very night, and would deliver Him to them when the night was right for it and not one hour before. He said all of it the way a doctor names a dose, no thunder in it anywhere, and the lawn took it the way you take a thing said kindly, which is to say it went quiet rather than loud.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah kept still under it. She was, by the terms of the evening, its subject \'97 thirty-four weeks of the One After the One rode out in front of her in oceanic velvet, and somewhere a screen was very likely carrying her face out to the fig trees again \'97 and she had worked out long ago that the safest thing to do with a thing said about you in front of half a million strangers is to receive it as though it were weather. She received it as weather.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then he said he would like to give them something that was not his, a thing the Dawkins had left lying around for anyone who wanted it, and he recited it plainly, without music, in the voice you would use for a child who had asked a real question.\
\pard\tx360\tx720\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\li1440\fi-360\sl264\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::2>
\f2\i We are going to die \uc0\u8232 That is what we must face \u8232 We are the lucky ones \u8232 Most never join the race.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::2><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 He let it sit the way he let everything sit, and the lawn sat with it. Then he went on, unhurried, a man in no danger of being interrupted.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The number of human beings \uc0\u8232 Who could be here in my place, \u8232 Outnumber all the sand grains \u8232 On the Sahara\'92s sandy face.
\f1\i0 \

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Untold unborn poets rhyming better than Keats \uc0\u8232 Countless bright minds Newton could not resist \u8232 The set of potential people and their possible feats \u8232 Exceeds the set of human beings that ever will exist.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Something moved across the dark lawn at that \'97 not a roar, nothing you could have sold as a roar, only half a million people privately running the sum and privately failing to make it come out, which is the correct answer. He brought it home gently.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>How dare we find ourselves whining, \uc0\u8232 At the prospect of being interred \u8232 Returning to the prior state \u8232 From which most have never stirred.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For a moment the lawn did nothing at all. Then somebody a long way back whooped, because a lawn cannot hold a silence any longer than it can hold its drink, and the ordinary sound came back up through the scaffolding and into Sarah\'92s bones, and the night went on being a concert.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For a while after that it was just that \'97 a set, good songs, the band at full quorum, the Colonel turning serene on his tether overhead and taking his percentage of the air, the crowd doing what a warm happy crowd does with music it likes. Then, near the end, before the last one, the High Elvis came back to the single microphone and said the only genuinely new thing said all night. The Great Teacher, he told them, had written them something. New. Never played \'97 not once, not anywhere, not even for the band until this afternoon. He named it the way you hand over a wrapped parcel. \'93Religious Minds,\'94 he said, and stepped back, and did not call anyone up to take a bow; and nobody on the lawn thought to wonder why the Great Teacher himself was not there to take one, because the band had already started.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>You\'92re caught in a church \uc0\u8232 You can\'92t walk out \u8232 Because you\'92re praying too much, baby \u8232 Why not condemn \u8232 What it\'92s doing to them \u8232 When they believe every word you say?
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was, from the first bar, a good song \'97 better than the ditty, better than anything the Sages had run them through, a proper hook with a proper ache under it, and the lawn did with it what a lawn does with a good hook at the end of a long warm day. It learned it. Not all of it and not well, but the part built to be learned came round again and half a million people who could not have quoted a word of the verse were ready for it \'97 not because they had understood a syllable of what it was accusing them of, but because it scanned, and it was catchy, and they were having a marvellous time.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>We can\'92t go on as Elvi \uc0\u8232 With religious minds (religious minds) \u8232 And we can\'92t build our freedom \u8232 With religious minds
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Beside Sarah the ermine chair had come alive. Eloise had carried the telemetry tablet in her lap for two hours and not once looked at it; now she looked at it, and her thumb was moving, and it was not telemetry she was after. She had heard the song the way she heard most things, which was as a quantity. The hook was already installed in half a million skulls on a single play \'97 the expensive part done for free. Every market would take it: the Ghetto for the tune, the Pale for the sneer, the Cloudborne for the novelty of an Elvi song that hated something. Licensing, the stream, the merch that wrote itself. She did the arithmetic cleanly and to the third decimal and arrived, and the one modifier she permitted herself she permitted because she meant it. \'93Number one by Tuesday,\'94 she said, to the stage, to the night, to nobody, in the mild customer-service register she kept for threats and forecasts alike. \'93Every market. Tax it.\'94 She set the tablet face-down on the ermine and went back to enjoying the song, which she was, enormously.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>So if an Elvis I know \uc0\u8232 Stops and comes by a show \u8232 Would I still see religion in their eyes?
\f1\i0 \

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Here you go again \uc0\u8232 Beg forgiveness again \u8232 You can\'92t see their words are lies, they\'92re lying \u8232 (Yes they\'92re lying)
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Far from any of this \'97 a whole city and a Layer and a great deal of engineered weather away, down where the towers kept the sun out on principle \'97 the same song was coming up through a concrete floor. It reached the Ghetto the way everything reached the Ghetto, second-hand and from above, bleeding down out of the Domain\'92s stream through somebody\'92s phone in the bar overhead and into the walk-in cooler beneath it, which Pastor Rex Humble had three weeks and a coat of whitewash ago consecrated as New Graceland. He had a small evening congregation for it \'97 a dozen of the modestly appointed, beige and bonneted, hip-still on the poured floor \'97 and he had the punch bowl out, cut glass, half a bowl deep in the dark curling harvest of shorn sideburns, and he had the Biblical Shears in his hand, orange-handled, blessed that morning, indistinguishable to the untutored eye from the sort of scissors that will also open a bag of chips. He was mid-benediction when the floor began to sing. He heard it for what he took it to be, which was the Evolutionaries\'92 racket carried on the night air, the apostate festival throwing its noise down into the one clean room left in the Ghetto, and he lifted his voice over the top of it without breaking stride, the accent thickening as it always did the moment he needed it to, sin rendered as a thing you could catch off a floor if you left the door open. He did not see. Behind him, in the second row, an Elvine in a beige sack had her eyes closed and her lips moving, soundless and careful, shaping we can\'92t go on, we can\'92t go on the way you shape a tune that has got into you before you have decided anything about it; and beside her a second pair of lips had quietly taken up the with religious minds; and Pastor Humble preached on over them, certain, sweating, saved, and wholly unaware that the floor of his church had begun, very softly, to learn the words.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Back on the platform the refrain came round again, and the lawn had it now, ragged and delighted and enormous, a whole graduating class of astronauts bellowing at the top of their lungs a line about not being able to go on the way they were going on, and going on exactly the way they were going on, and enjoying it enormously.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With religious minds
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It reached Sarah cleanly, the way a thing meant for you reaches you across a room full of people it is not meant for. She had been the Holy Momma all night and had worn it as weather; but the song was not asking after the Rollers and their heaven, or not only. It was asking after this \'97 after the ocean of upturned faces out there singing her unborn Son at her, kneeling to a belly they could not have put a name to, worshipping the accident of her every bit as hard as any bonneted Elvine ever worshipped a floor \'97 and it was asking whether that was, in the end, a different thing from the church it said you couldn\'92t walk out of; and she found she had no answer ready, and that the absence of one was arriving at exactly the size advertised. Inside her the One After the One turned, a slow oceanic roll timed to nothing, and her hand went to her belly before she had been consulted about it. She retrieved it. She put her face back where she had left it and kept it there, which was, and had always been, the most she could manage against anything that turned up the actual size it had promised. No one saw. Beside her Eloise was enjoying the song. Below her the lawn was enjoying the song. She sat at the still centre of the one accusation on the entire Domain that had been addressed, precisely, to her, and schooled her face, and let it be sung.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Oh, let our kind survive \uc0\u8232 We\'92ll wipe the tears from their eyes \u8232 Let\'92s please let a bad thing die \u8232 When honey, you know I\'92ve never prayed with you
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And then it was over, the way songs are over \'97 a last chord held past its use and let go, the band lifting their hands, the High Elvis stepping back from the microphone he had lifted and setting it down again on its stand as gently as he had taken it up. The lawn did what a lawn does at the end of a good last song on a warm night: it made an enormous grateful noise, meant nothing by it beyond the noise, and began almost at once to think about the long walk to the travelators. Nothing came down out of the Bubble. Nothing detonated on the grass. The Colonel turned on his tether and puffed a ring of cigar smoke up at the first clear stars, and out on the perimeter the garlanded tanks stood exactly as garlanded as before, and the screens, having no face left worth throwing thirty feet high, went back to black. It had not been a revelation. It had been a very good concert, and it was ending on its own weightless terms, the way the best of them do. On the platform Sarah sat beside the empty throne in the settling roar, her hand back in her lap where it belonged, still the Holy Momma, still invincible, still \'97 had anyone asked, and no one did \'97 perfectly all right; and the night, having decided nothing, let her be.\

\f0\fs24 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1>}